e:article-doi-10.3390_ma14195738 a schema:ScholarlyArticle ;
    schema:contentUrl "runs/literature/papers/alloy/2026-09-26_extract-batch2/10.3390_ma14195738.txt" ;
    schema:identifier "https://doi.org/10.3390/ma14195738" ;
    schema:name "Electrical Resistivity and Tensile Strength Relationship in Heat-Treated All Aluminum Alloy Wire Conductors" ;
    al:notReported "The paper calls the 130 C treatment 'annealing'; it raised the strength (precipitation hardening), stored as ArtificialAgeing at review",
        "YTS and EL not reported anywhere in the paper; only UTS (tensile strength) is given",
        "electrical resistivity values (e.g. 35 nOhm.m as-drawn, 29.5 nOhm.m final) are not part of UTS/YTS/EL and are not recorded",
        "temper not stated for any condition (as-drawn / annealed without solution treatment); temper left out for all samples",
        "tensile strength of the 1.7 mm wires at each annealing condition (130-250 C, 10-360 min) is only in Figure 2; only the as-drawn value (327 MPa) and the approximate final value (around 120 MPa at 230/250 C) are stated in text",
        "tensile strength of the 3.5 mm wires at each annealing condition is only in Figure 3; only the as-drawn value (333 MPa), the 130 C/3 h peak (365 MPa) and the 250 C value (180 MPa) are stated in text",
        "the 180 MPa value for wires annealed at 250 C is given in the Discussion without an explicit holding time; 6 h (the maximum treatment time) was used for the step",
        "the 365 MPa value is described as a strength peak reached after three hours at 130 C; the exact condition is 130 C for 3 h",
        "the 9.5 mm rod's prior processing (casting/homogenization) is not described; only 'produced commercially' is stated" .

